
# Port plan: native search engine behind the gn4 Engine seam

* assigned: alex
* tags: gn4, search, rust
* status: proposed

## Goal

Move gn4 /search from forwarding to gn3 to a native Rust engine
behind the Engine seam (branch native-search-seam, under review),
reading the existing xapian index through the patched xapian-rs fork:

=> https://github.com/Alexanderlacuna/xapian-rs Patched xapian-rs fork: checked-parse API (typed parser errors instead of panics), processor declines map to MatchNothing, f64 range bounds, cxx pinned for the 1.85 toolchain. Its test suite runs under the pinned guix toolchain (rust 1.85.1, libxapian 1.4.29).

Definition of done: the parity harness (branch parity-harness) scores
54/54 with the native engine behind the route, plus the usual gates
(build, tests, clippy, guix) and review sign-off.

## The graded contract

The parity harness grades 54 shapes recorded from live production
(2026-10-05), now committed as a reduced corpus that runs hermetically
in ~20 seconds on any checkout:

* 200-match shapes: status + full match-set equality + top-10 overlap
  against the recorded fixtures.
* Recorded parser errors (`alcohol AND`, `mean:abc..def`) grade as
  gn4 422 invalid_query through the existing error mapping.
* The recorded 500 for a malformed location (`Hs:foo:1M`) grades as
  gn4 502 backend_error -- recorded production behavior, fixture wins.
* `a&&b`, `alcohol &&`, `position:` are recorded as 200-with-hits
  (production accepts them under libxapian 1.4.29) and grade as match
  shapes.

Fixtures are the grading truth. If the owner changes a contract, the
affected fixtures re-baseline and the harness enforces the new one --
decisions land as single revertible commits, not silent drift.

## TODOs

Each stage is one or a few modular commits, each compiling, tested,
revertible on its own, and gated by named harness shapes:

* C0 merge prerequisite: seam + harness branches onto one base.
  Gate: forwarding route still 54/54 (nothing changed for users).
* C1 wiring: git dependency on the fork (rev-pinned), index-path
  config (`XAPIAN_DB_PATH`, mirroring gn3's own key), engine-selection
  switch (forwarding stays available as the fallback). Gate:
  build/tests green, no shape delta.
* C2 index access: engine opens the recorded index snapshot
  read-only. Gate: recorded-count spot checks against the fixtures.
* C3 the engine, in six incremental commits, each turning on its
  named shapes:
  * C3.1 core + route flip: stemmer, checked parse, XT type filter,
    full-match-set retrieval, result shaping.
  * C3.2 boolean prefixes: author, species, group (lowercased),
    tissue, dataset, symbol, chr, peakchr.
  * C3.3 probabilistic prefixes + aliases: description, rif, wiki.
  * C3.4 numeric ranges: mean, peak, peakmb, additive, year.
  * C3.5 position/pos SI-suffix processor.
  * C3.6 Hs/Mm location processors, including the recorded
    malformed-chromosome 500 -> 502 mechanics.
* C4 full floor: 54/54 + review sign-off.

## Open decisions

* 422-vs-500 for malformed input: proposal recorded in
  gn4-search-native.gmi -- all malformed query input -> 422 with a
  typed body, one consistent contract instead of inherited crashes.
  The harness enforces whichever is chosen; the swap is one
  revertible commit.
* Hs liftover: implement (the chain file already lives in gn3) or
  accept the documented difference; isolated in one commit either way.
* Engine choice: native xapian through the patched bindings
  (recommended on recorded evidence -- same index, same grammar,
  near-automatic parity floor) versus tantivy (possible behind the
  same seam, at a higher cost: indexer rebuild + a 142-package
  dependency tree).

Mechanical defaults already chosen, visible and revertible: malformed
position values -> empty match (not a crash); config names as above.

## Already in place

* The fork: live, tested, documented (link above).
* The harness: branch parity-harness, calibrated 54/54 against the
  forwarding route, fails loudly by construction (negative controls).
* The seam: branch native-search-seam, under review.
* A local development index (47k documents from the 2025-02 public
  snapshot, built with the production indexer) supports engine
  development before the production snapshot is available;
  recorded-count gates still need the real index.

